encyclopedia :stratum_exchange do
  name "Stratum Exchange"
  kind :phenomenon
  subkind :physical_phenomenon
  status :complete
  topics :"fluid-reality", :mystery, :danger, :archives
  prevalence :rare
  appears_when all: { place: [:displacement_zone] }
  summary "A stratum exchange is the rarest and tidiest of the displacement events: two layers of a structure trade places overnight — third floor for fifth, cellar for attic — each arriving intact, furnished, and exactly where the other used to be. The law is ready for it, and the residents never quite are."
  medium "Layered structures in displacement country: habs, towers, terraced settlements"
  nature "A clean positional swap of two strata, complete with contents, recorded without witnesses to the transition itself"
  hazard "Every connection between the swapped layers and the building — stairs, pipes, habits — is wrong by exactly the swap, and the first hour's injuries are all people using yesterday's map"

  descriptive_identity(
    signs: "The building wakes up wrong: the stair that led to the loom room leads to the " \
           "cold store, the windows on the fifth show the third's view, and the residents " \
           "assemble in the street to count floors aloud like children doing sums.",
    effects: "The swapped strata arrive whole — furniture, stores, sleeping cats, the dust " \
             "in the corners — and every recorded exchange has been pairwise, complete, " \
             "and unwitnessed in the act, including by people asleep in the layers " \
             "exchanged.",
    hazards: "Services connect by position: pipes feed the wrong kitchens, the third " \
             "floor's weight sits where the fifth's was engineered for, and displaced-room " \
             "law's tidiest chapter meets its messiest mornings."
  )

  prose <<~PROSE
    Displacement country's catastrophes are mostly ragged — rooms arriving in the middle of other rooms, geometry meeting geometry at a lie of an angle. The stratum exchange is the exception that unsettles people more: two whole layers of a structure trade places, cleanly, completely, and overnight. The third floor is the fifth. The cellar is the attic, potatoes and all. Each stratum arrives intact and level, joined to its new neighbors with seamless walls and working doors, as though the building had always been stacked that way — and every connection that runs by position rather than by layer is wrong by exactly the swap. Xyloathax's registry, which keeps the deepest exchange records, lists eleven certain events; the Gray Line cordon adds four; and the phenomenon's tidiness is its signature in every one: pairwise, complete, unwitnessed in the act, including by the people asleep inside the layers as they moved.

    The morning after has a shape the displacement services drill for. Residents wake to right rooms in wrong places, the building's users run on yesterday's map — the first hour's injuries are almost all stairs taken on habit — and the braced-and-surveyed routine rolls out under #{encyclopedia_ref :displaced_room_law, "displaced-room law"}'s tidiest chapter, since an exchange, unlike an arrival, changes ownership of nothing: everyone still owns their stratum, merely elsewhere. The services' checklists are correspondingly domestic. Re-map the stairs, re-plumb by layer, walk the elderly through the new stack twice a day for a month, and log everything for the surveys, whose standing question the eleven-times-tidy record keeps sharpening: what kind of accident is never messy?
  PROSE

  cue "The whole building's residents stand in the street at dawn counting windows aloud, and the third floor's flower boxes bloom two storeys higher than yesterday."
  cue "Inside, a resident opens the stair door on ingrained habit, stops at a cold store that should be a loom room, and stands there recalculating with a hand still on the latch."
  affordance "An exchange moves everything and destroys nothing — the swapped strata arrive whole and habitable, ownership follows the layer under law, and the displacement services' recovery is re-mapping rather than rescue."
  pressure "Everything positional is wrong by exactly the swap — pipes, loads, habits, the mental map of every resident — and the tidy record's implication, argued in every survey since the first event, is that the mess is being handled by something."
  variation "Xyloathax's eleven certain exchanges cluster in its Bloom-caught quarter and are commemorated by the residents with floor-warming parties in both directions."
  variation "The Gray Line's four events include the one double exchange on record — two swaps, two seasons apart, restoring the original order — which the registry files with the annotation 'resolved,' over the surveyors' written objection."
end
